response:
 First, notice that vector $\mathbf{b}$ must be a linear combination of vectors $\mathbf{a}$ and $\mathbf{c}.$  Thus,  $\mathbf{b} = t \mathbf{a} + (1-t) \mathbf{c}$ for some value of $t.$  Considering the projection of $\mathbf{b}$ onto its unit length allows us to obtain two new vectors $\dot{\mathbf{a}}$ and $\dot{\mathbf{c}}$ which are parallel and whose sum is $\mathbf{b}.$   That is, \[\mathbf{b} = \frac{\mathbf{a} \cdot \mathbf{b}}{\mathbf{a} \cdot \mathbf{a}} \mathbf{a} + \frac{\mathbf{c} \cdot \mathbf{b}}{\mathbf{c} \cdot \mathbf{c}} \mathbf{c} \Rightarrow \mathbf{b} = (\mathbf{a} \cdot \mathbf{b}) \dot{\mathbf{a}} + (\mathbf{c} \cdot \mathbf{b}) \dot{\mathbf{c}}.\]but, $\|\mathbf{b}\| \|\dot{\mathbf{a}}\| = \mathbf{a} \cdot \dot{\mathbf{a}}$ and  $\|\mathbf{b}\| \|\dot{\mathbf{c}}\| = \mathbf{c} \cdot \dot{\mathbf{c}}.$ Therefore, \[]\mathbf{b}\| = (\mathbf{a} \cdot \mathbf{b}) \frac{\|\mathbf{a}\|}{\mathbf{a} \cdot \mathbf{a}} + (\mathbf{c} \cdot \mathbf{b}) \frac{\|\mathbf{c}\|}{\mathbf{c} \cdot \mathbf{c}} = \frac{\mathbf{a}}{\|\mathbf{a}\|} \cdot \mathbf{b} + \frac{\mathbf{c}}{\|\mathbf{c}\|} \cdot \mathbf{b} = (\mathbf{a} + \mathbf{c}) \cdot \frac{\mathbf{b}}{\|\mathbf{b}\|} = \mathbf{a} + \mathbf{c}.\] [asy]
